  "body": "**What happens:** `cargo xtask gate batch` (and every gate built on it: merge trains, post-merge checks) runs its ~620 steps strictly one after another (`xtask/src/exec.rs`, \"the one-at-a-time loop\"). Each step compiles and links one test binary, then runs it. Linking a large Rust test binary uses about one core.\n\n**Evidence (2026-11-19, merge train on a 16-core Hetzner ccx43, main 409c0b5ab + one PR):**\n- 1 h 20 min wall time; load average 2.7-2.8 on 16 cores for most of the run; the provider's CPU graph sits at ~100-300 % of 1600 %.\n- At the moment checked: step 623 (`app suites: pocket3d_reach`), one `rustc` at 100 %, nothing else busy.\n- `CAMCRAFT_GATE_JOBS=16` was set; it scales `-j` and `--test-threads` inside a step, but the steps themselves do not overlap.\n\nThe release tier already has parallel lanes (#2057, the longest-first queue of #2461), which took the release gate from 114.6 to 52.7 min. The batch tier does not use them.\n\n**Proposal (two parts, either helps):**\n1. **One parallel prebuild:** before the steps, build every test binary the plan needs in one cargo call (`cargo test --no-run -j N` over the planned packages/targets, with the same profile and features the steps use, so the steps reuse the artifacts instead of relinking one by one).\n2. **Lanes for batch:** run the batch steps through the release queue's lanes (longest first, sharing the job budget), so several test binaries run at once.\n\nKeep what the gate guarantees today: every step still runs and is logged, a red step still fails the gate, VOID detection is unchanged.\n\n**Done means:** `cargo xtask gate batch` on a 16-core host uses most of its cores during the build phase, and its wall time drops substantially against the same commit (measure before/after on the same machine; numbers in the PR).\n\nRelated: #2461, #2057, #2556, #2557.\n",
